#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:2
msgid "Callable Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:4
msgid ""
"In Xonsh, a Python function can be registered as a shell command (alias). "
"The function declares which arguments it needs, and Xonsh fills them "
"automatically based on parameter names. When a function runs as an alias, "
"Xonsh redirects ``sys.stdout`` and ``sys.stderr`` inside it, making it "
"possible to capture all output that happens within the function — including "
"``print()`` calls and subprocess commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:13
msgid "Signature Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:15
msgid ""
"Callable aliases receive arguments matched by name. You can request any "
"combination of the following parameters in any order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:22
msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:23
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:24
msgid "``args``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:25
msgid "List of strings — command-line arguments passed to the alias."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:26
msgid "``stdin``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:27
msgid "File-like object for reading piped input, or ``None`` if no pipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:28
msgid "``stdout``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:29
msgid "File-like object for writing standard output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:30
msgid "``stderr``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:31
msgid "File-like object for writing standard error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:32
msgid "``spec``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:33
msgid "``SubprocSpec`` object with details about how the command is being run."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:34
msgid "``stack``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:35
msgid ""
"List of ``FrameInfo`` namedtuples from the call site (only computed if "
"requested)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:37
msgid "``alias_name``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:38
msgid "The name under which the alias was registered."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:39
msgid "``called_alias_name``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:40
msgid ""
"The name actually used to invoke the alias (may differ if one alias points "
"to another)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:42
msgid "``env``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:43
msgid ""
"A local environment overlay dict. Values set here shadow the global env "
"during alias execution and are visible to subprocesses. Removed "
"automatically when the alias exits. When you set `env['VAR']=1` it's like "
"`with @.env.swap(VAR=1)` for the rest of the callable alias code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:48
msgid "You only need to declare the parameters you actually use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:50
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _hello():\n"
"      print('hello')\n"
"\n"
"@ hello\n"
"hello\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _upper(args, stdin=None):\n"
"      text = stdin.read() if stdin else ' '.join(args)\n"
"      print(text.upper())\n"
"\n"
"@ upper hello world\n"
"HELLO WORLD\n"
"@ echo quiet | upper\n"
"QUIET\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _shout(args, stdin=None, stdout=None):\n"
"      \"\"\"Read stdin, write to stdout.\"\"\"\n"
"      for line in stdin or []:\n"
"          stdout.write(line.upper())\n"
"\n"
"@ echo 'hello\\nworld' | shout\n"
"HELLO\n"
"WORLD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:81
msgid "Alias Name and Called Alias Name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:83
msgid ""
"When one alias points to another, it can be useful to know how the alias was "
"invoked. The ``alias_name`` and ``called_alias_name`` parameters provide "
"this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:86
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register('groot')\n"
"  def _groot(alias_name=None, called_alias_name=None):\n"
"      print(f'I am {alias_name}! You called me {called_alias_name}.')\n"
"@ aliases['tree'] = 'groot'\n"
"@ groot\n"
"I am groot! You called me groot.\n"
"@ tree\n"
"I am groot! You called me tree."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:97
msgid ""
"``alias_name`` is set at registration time and never changes. "
"``called_alias_name`` is set at each invocation and reflects the name the "
"user actually typed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:103
msgid "Local Environment Overlay"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:105
msgid ""
"The ``env`` parameter provides a local environment overlay. Values set in "
"``env`` shadow the global environment during alias execution — both for "
"Xonsh ``$VAR`` reads and for subprocesses. When the alias exits, the overlay "
"is removed and the global environment is unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:110
msgid ""
"Direct writes to ``$VAR`` or ``@.env`` modify the global environment as "
"usual and persist after the alias exits:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:113
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _ca(env=None):\n"
"      $GLOBAL = 2\n"
"      echo GLOBAL before overlay = $GLOBAL\n"
"      env['GLOBAL'] = 1\n"
"      echo GLOBAL after overlay = $GLOBAL\n"
"      printenv GLOBAL\n"
"\n"
"@ ca\n"
"GLOBAL before overlay = 2\n"
"GLOBAL after overlay = 1\n"
"1\n"
"@ echo GLOBAL after alias = $GLOBAL\n"
"GLOBAL after alias = 2"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:130
msgid ""
"Inside the alias, ``$GLOBAL`` returns ``1`` (overlay has priority) and "
"subprocesses see ``GLOBAL=1`` in their environment. After the alias exits, "
"``$GLOBAL`` is back to ``2`` (the global value set by ``$GLOBAL = 2``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:136
msgid "Return Command Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:138
msgid ""
"The ``@aliases.return_command`` decorator creates aliases that return a new "
"command to execute instead of running it themselves. The body of the alias "
"can run its own commands first, then return the command Xonsh should execute "
"on its behalf."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:143
msgid "The alias may return its result in either of two forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:145
msgid ""
"**1. A non-empty list** — just the command tokens. The returned command has "
"no env overlay; if you need to set env vars for it you must use the dict "
"form below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:149
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _rca(args):\n"
"      return ['xonsh', '-c', 'echo hello']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:156
msgid ""
"**2. A dict** with a required ``\"cmd\"`` key (non-empty list of tokens) and "
"an optional ``\"env\"`` key (dict) — the command tokens plus an env overlay "
"that applies **only** to the returned command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:160
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _rca(args):\n"
"      return {\n"
"          'cmd': ['xonsh', '-c', 'echo $RETURNED'],\n"
"          'env': {'RETURNED': 'set_by_dict'},\n"
"      }\n"
"\n"
"@ rca\n"
"set_by_dict\n"
"@ $RETURNED\n"
"Unknown environment variable: $RETURNED"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:175
msgid ""
"The ``env=`` kwarg of a ``return_command`` alias behaves exactly like the "
"``env=`` kwarg of an ordinary callable alias: it is a **local overlay active "
"only during the function body**. Mutating it affects commands the alias runs "
"inline (e.g. via ``$[...]``, ``!()``, or subprocess syntax), but it does "
"**not** flow to the returned command. To set env for the returned command, "
"the alias must use the dict form above."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:182
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _rca(args, env=None):\n"
"      env['BODY_ONLY'] = 'visible_inside'\n"
"      # A subprocess spawned here sees BODY_ONLY=visible_inside\n"
"      $[env | grep BODY_ONLY]\n"
"      # But the returned command does NOT — it has no overlay at all.\n"
"      return ['env']"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:193
msgid ""
"Direct writes to ``$VAR`` or ``@.env`` still modify the global environment "
"and persist after the alias exits, as for any callable alias."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:196
msgid ""
"The following example exercises all four env-flow paths of a "
"``return_command`` alias in one place: the ``env=`` kwarg overlay (body-"
"only), a direct global write (persists), a dict-return ``\"env\"`` overlay "
"(applies only to the returned command), and the global value that flows "
"through both."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:201
msgid ""
"@ $GLOBAL = 1\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.return_command\n"
"  def _rca(env):\n"
"      # ``env`` is the body-scoped overlay (introduced in 0.23.0).\n"
"      # Mutating it affects commands the alias runs inline.\n"
"      env['LOCAL'] = 1\n"
"      xonsh -c @('echo g=$GLOBAL l=$LOCAL')\n"
"      # Direct write to the global env — persists after the alias exits.\n"
"      $GLOBAL = 2\n"
"      return {\n"
"          'cmd': ['xonsh', '-c', 'echo g=$GLOBAL l=$LOCAL'],\n"
"          'env': {'LOCAL': 2},\n"
"      }\n"
"\n"
"@ rca\n"
"# xonsh inside the alias body:\n"
"#   g=1  from the global $GLOBAL set before the alias\n"
"#   l=1  from the ``env=`` kwarg overlay (body-scoped)\n"
"g=1 l=1\n"
"\n"
"# the returned xonsh command:\n"
"#   g=2  from the direct write ``$GLOBAL = 2`` in the body\n"
"#   l=2  from the dict-return ``\"env\"`` overlay\n"
"g=2 l=2\n"
"\n"
"@ $LOCAL\n"
"# the body overlay is gone,\n"
"# and the dict overlay only\n"
"# applied to the returned command\n"
"Unknown environment variable: $LOCAL\n"
"\n"
"@ $GLOBAL\n"
"2    # the direct write persisted"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:241
msgid "Return Values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:243
msgid "Callable aliases can return values in several forms:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:245
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _ret0():\n"
"      return 0  # integer return code (0 = success)\n"
"\n"
"@ ret0\n"
"@ $LAST_RETURN_CODE\n"
"0\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _ret_str():\n"
"      return 'hello from return'\n"
"\n"
"@ $(ret-str)\n"
"'hello from return\\n'\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _ret_tuple():\n"
"      return ('out text', 'err text', 1)\n"
"\n"
"@ !(ret-tuple)\n"
"err text\n"
"CommandPipeline(returncode=1, output='out text\\n', errors='err text\\n')"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:270
msgid ""
"Anything printed with ``print()`` inside the function is also captured "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:275
msgid "Capturing and Stream Redirection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:277
msgid ""
"Inside a callable alias, ``sys.stdout`` and ``sys.stderr`` are temporarily "
"replaced with the alias's own streams. The ``stdout`` and ``stderr`` "
"function arguments point to the **same** redirected streams. This means that "
"``print()`` and ``print(file=stdout)`` are equivalent — both write to the "
"alias's captured output, not directly to the terminal:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:283
msgid ""
"# These are all the same stream inside a callable alias:\n"
"sys.stdout  is  stdout      # True\n"
"sys.stderr  is  stderr      # True\n"
"print(\"x\")                  # goes to alias stdout\n"
"print(\"x\", file=stdout)    # same\n"
"stdout.write(\"x\\n\")        # same"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:292
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _demo(args, stdout=None, stderr=None):\n"
"      print(\"via print()\")\n"
"      stdout.write(\"via stdout.write()\\n\")\n"
"      echo \"via subprocess\"\n"
"      print(\"error\", file=stderr)\n"
"\n"
"@ output = $(_demo)\n"
"error\n"
"@ print(output)\n"
"via print()\n"
"via stdout.write()\n"
"via subprocess"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:308
msgid ""
"Here is a more complete example showing how different output methods behave "
"under capture:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:311
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _printer(args, stdin, stdout, stderr):\n"
"      \"\"\"Ultimate printer.\"\"\"\n"
"      print(\"print out\")\n"
"      print(\"print err\", file=@.imp.sys.stderr)\n"
"\n"
"      print(\"print out alias stdout\", file=stdout)\n"
"      print(\"print err alias stderr\", file=stderr)\n"
"\n"
"      echo @(\"echo out\")\n"
"      echo @(\"echo err\") o>e\n"
"\n"
"      $(echo @(\"$() echo out\"))\n"
"      $(echo @(\"$() echo err\") o>e)\n"
"\n"
"      !(echo @(\"!() echo out\"))\n"
"      !(echo @(\"!() echo err\") o>e)\n"
"\n"
"      ![echo @(\"![] echo out\")]\n"
"      ![echo @(\"![] echo err\") o>e]\n"
"\n"
"      $[echo @(\"$[] echo out\")]\n"
"      $[echo @(\"$[] echo err\") o>e]\n"
"\n"
"      execx('echo \"execx echo out\"')\n"
"      execx('echo \"execx echo err\" o>e')\n"
"\n"
"@ $(printer)\n"
"print err\n"
"print err alias stderr\n"
"echo err\n"
"$() echo err\n"
"![] echo err\n"
"$[] echo out\n"
"$[] echo err\n"
"execx echo err\n"
"'print out\\necho out\\nprint out alias stdout\\n![] echo out\\nexecx echo "
"out\\n'\n"
"\n"
"@ !(printer)\n"
"$() echo err\n"
"$[] echo out\n"
"$[] echo err\n"
"CommandPipeline(\n"
"  returncode=0,\n"
"  output='print out\\necho out\\nprint out alias stdout\\n![] echo "
"out\\nexecx echo out\\n',\n"
"  errors='print err\\necho err\\nprint err alias stderr\\n![] echo "
"err\\nexecx echo err\\n'\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:361
msgid ""
"When the alias is captured with ``$()`` or ``!()``, its stdout is collected. "
"When called uncaptured (bare command), output goes to the terminal as usual. "
"The ``stderr`` argument and ``sys.stderr`` are also redirected — use ``sys."
"__stderr__`` if you need to bypass capture:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:366
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _loud(args, stdin=None):\n"
"      print(\"this is captured\")\n"
"      print(\"this goes to real terminal\", file=@.imp.sys.__stderr__)\n"
"\n"
"@ output = $(_loud)\n"
"this goes to real terminal\n"
"@ print(output)\n"
"this is captured"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:380
msgid "Streams"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:382
msgid ""
"Inside a callable alias Xonsh replaces ``sys.stdout`` and ``sys.stderr`` "
"with the alias's own streams.  The ``stdout`` and ``stderr`` function "
"arguments point to the **same** redirected streams.  So bare ``print()`` "
"just works — in pipes, in capture, everywhere:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:387
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"def _greet(args):\n"
"    print(\"hello\")       # goes to pipe, capture, or terminal — wherever "
"needed\n"
"\n"
"# all of these work:\n"
"greet                     # prints to terminal\n"
"greet | grep hello        # piped to grep\n"
"output = $(greet)         # captured into variable"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:398
msgid ""
"The ``stdout`` / ``stderr`` arguments give you the **same stream as a file "
"object**, which is useful when you need to pass it to functions that accept "
"a file argument, or when you need ``.write()`` for finer control:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:402
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"def _json_dump(args, stdout=None):\n"
"    import json\n"
"    data = {\"key\": \"value\"}\n"
"    json.dump(data, stdout)          # json.dump needs a file object\n"
"    stdout.write(\"\\n\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:411
msgid "**Reading from** ``stdin``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:413
msgid ""
"``stdin`` is ``None`` when the alias is called standalone, and a readable "
"stream when piped into.  This is the one argument you **must** use "
"explicitly — there is no automatic redirection of ``sys.stdin`` for aliases:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:417
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"def _upper(args, stdin=None):\n"
"    if stdin is not None:\n"
"        for line in stdin:\n"
"            print(line.strip().upper())"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:425
msgid ""
"@ echo hello | upper\n"
"HELLO"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:431
msgid "**Binary data — bypassing universal newlines**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:433
msgid ""
"``stdin``, ``stdout``, and ``stderr`` are text streams (``io."
"TextIOWrapper``). Reading them applies *universal newlines* to incoming "
"bytes: ``\\r\\n`` and lone ``\\r`` are translated to ``\\n``. That is "
"convenient for text but can corrupts binary data flowing through the alias — "
"every CR is silently rewritten."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:439
msgid ""
"To pass bytes through unchanged, use the underlying ``.buffer`` on both ends:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:442
msgid ""
"@aliases.register\n"
"def _passthru(args, stdin=None, stdout=None):\n"
"    \"\"\"Copy stdin to stdout byte-for-byte.\"\"\"\n"
"    if stdin is None:\n"
"        return\n"
"    for chunk in iter(lambda: stdin.buffer.read(65536), b\"\"):\n"
"        stdout.buffer.write(chunk)\n"
"    stdout.buffer.flush()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:453
msgid ""
"@ cat /usr/bin/python | passthru > /tmp/copy\n"
"@ # bytes preserved exactly — diff /usr/bin/python /tmp/copy is empty"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:458
msgid ""
"This applies symmetrically: ``stdout.write(text)`` goes through the text "
"layer (and on Windows translates ``\\n`` to ``\\r\\n``), while ``stdout."
"buffer.write(b\"...\")`` writes raw bytes. Reach for ``.buffer`` whenever "
"the alias is a passthrough or otherwise binary-aware — ``cat``-likes, "
"hashing, compression, image/audio filters, ``ssh``-style streaming."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:465
msgid ""
"Reading a file directly with ``open(path, \"rb\")`` is also unaffected: the "
"text layer only applies to ``stdin``/``stdout``/``stderr``. So ``mycat file "
"> out`` (no upstream pipe) is safe; ``something | mycat > out`` needs "
"``stdin.buffer`` to stay byte-clean."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:472 ../../../callable_aliases.rst:535
msgid "Threading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:474
msgid ""
"By default, callable aliases run in a **separate thread** so they can be "
"used in pipelines and run in the background. This is usually what you want."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:477
msgid ""
"However, some aliases need to run on the **main thread** — for example, "
"interactive tools (vim, less, htop), debuggers, profilers, or anything that "
"modifies terminal state. Use the ``unthreadable`` decorator to force "
"foreground execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:482
msgid ""
"Threading and capturability can also be controlled at call time using the "
"``@thread``, ``@unthread`` command decorators. These override the function's "
"decorators for a single invocation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:486
msgid ""
"@ @unthread my-alias    # force main thread for this call\n"
"@ @thread my-alias      # force background thread for this call"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:491
msgid "To set it permanently on the function, use the Python decorator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:493
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.unthreadable\n"
"  def _vi(args, stdin=None):\n"
"      vim @(args)\n"
"\n"
"@ vi myfile.txt\n"
"# opens vim on the main thread"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:505
msgid "Capturability"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:507
msgid ""
"By default, callable aliases are **capturable** — their output can be "
"collected with ``$()``, ``!()``, or piped to another command. This is how "
"most aliases work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:511
msgid ""
"Some aliases launch interactive programs (editors, pagers, TUI apps) that "
"take over the terminal. These must not be captured, or the program will not "
"display correctly. Use ``@aliases.uncapturable``, typically together with "
"``@aliases.unthreadable``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:516
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  @aliases.uncapturable\n"
"  @aliases.unthreadable\n"
"  def _edit(args, stdin=None):\n"
"      vim @(args)\n"
"\n"
"@ edit myfile.txt\n"
"# opens vim directly in the terminal"
msgstr ""

