#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:544
msgid "Click CLI Integration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:546
msgid ""
"If the `click <https://click.palletsprojects.com/>`_ package is installed, "
"xonsh exposes two helpers on the ``aliases`` object:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:549
msgid ""
"``aliases.click`` — the ``click`` module itself, for decorating functions "
"with ``@aliases.click.option(...)``, ``@aliases.click.argument(...)``, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:551
msgid ""
"``aliases.register_click_command`` — a decorator that registers a click "
"command as a Xonsh alias."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:554
msgid ""
"Both are loaded lazily on first access — sessions that never touch click "
"don't pay the import cost, and nothing breaks on systems where click is not "
"installed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:558
msgid ""
"@ @aliases.register_click_command\n"
"  @aliases.click.option('--count', default=1, help='Number of greetings.')\n"
"  @aliases.click.option('--name', help='The person to greet.')\n"
"  def _hello(ctx, count, name):\n"
"      \"\"\"Simple program that greets NAME for a total of COUNT times."
"\"\"\"\n"
"      for i in range(count):\n"
"          print(name, file=ctx.stdout)\n"
"\n"
"@ hello --count 3 --name World\n"
"World\n"
"World\n"
"World"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:573
msgid "The decorator mirrors the calling conventions of ``@aliases.register``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:575
msgid ""
"@aliases.register_click_command              # bare, name from function\n"
"@aliases.register_click_command()            # empty parentheses, same "
"thing\n"
"@aliases.register_click_command(\"my-name\")   # explicit alias name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:581
msgid ""
"The function's first argument is a ``click.Context`` subclass that carries "
"every standard Xonsh alias parameter from `Signature Parameters`_ as an "
"attribute of the same name — ``ctx.stdin``, ``ctx.stdout``, ``ctx.stderr``, "
"``ctx.spec``, ``ctx.stack``, ``ctx.decorators``, ``ctx.alias_name``, ``ctx."
"called_alias_name``, ``ctx.env``. The only exception is ``args``, which is "
"exposed as ``ctx.alias_args`` to avoid clashing with the built-in ``click."
"Context.args`` that ``click`` uses for option parsing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:589
msgid ""
"The ``click`` module itself is also attached as ``ctx.click``, so callbacks "
"can call ``ctx.click.echo(...)``, ``ctx.click.secho(...)``, etc. without a "
"separate ``import click``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:593
msgid ""
"Use these when a click command needs the underlying Xonsh streams or "
"environment overlay — for example, ``print(text, file=ctx.stdout)`` writes "
"to the alias's captured output the same way a regular callable alias does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:597
msgid ""
"Tab completion is wired up automatically: option flags, ``click.Choice`` "
"option values, positional ``click.Choice`` arguments, and sub-commands of a "
"``click.Group`` are all suggested without any extra configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:601
msgid ""
"@ hello --<TAB>\n"
"--count --help --name"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:608
msgid "String Aliases and ExecAlias"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:610
msgid ""
"When you assign a string to an alias, Xonsh stores it in one of two ways "
"depending on the content."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:613
msgid ""
"A **simple string** like ``\"ls -la\"`` is split into a list of tokens and "
"stored as ``[\"ls\", \"-la\"]``. This is equivalent to assigning the list "
"directly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:617
msgid ""
"@ aliases['ll'] = 'ls -la'\n"
"@ aliases['ll'] = ['ls', '-la']   # same thing"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:622
msgid ""
"A string that contains Xonsh expressions (``@()``, ``$()``), pipes (``|``), "
"redirections (``>``, ``<``), or logical operators (``&&``, ``||``) cannot be "
"represented as a simple list — it needs to be compiled and executed as Xonsh "
"code. Xonsh wraps such strings in an ``ExecAlias``, which is a callable "
"alias under the hood:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:628
msgid ""
"@ aliases['answer'] = 'echo @(21+21)'\n"
"@ answer\n"
"42\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['findpy'] = 'ls | grep $arg0'\n"
"@ findpy .py\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['combo'] = 'echo start && echo end'\n"
"@ combo\n"
"start\n"
"end"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:644
msgid "Arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:646
msgid ""
"When an ``ExecAlias`` runs, the arguments passed to it are available as "
"temporary environment variables:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:649
msgid "``$args`` — the full list of arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:650
msgid "``$arg0``, ``$arg1``, … — individual positional arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:652
msgid ""
"These variables exist only while the alias body is running and are removed "
"afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:655
msgid ""
"@ aliases['greet'] = 'echo Hello, $arg0!'\n"
"@ greet World\n"
"Hello, World!\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['piu'] = 'pip install -U @($args)'\n"
"@ piu xonsh prompt_toolkit\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['cdls'] = 'cd $arg0 && ls'\n"
"@ cdls /tmp"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:667
msgid ""
"Arguments are **not** passed automatically — you need to use ``$args`` or "
"``$arg<n>`` explicitly. If you don't reference them, they are ignored:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:670
msgid ""
"@ aliases['noargs'] = 'echo @(\"arguments are ignored\")'\n"
"@ noargs 1 2 3\n"
"arguments are ignored\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['withargs'] = 'echo the arguments are: @($args)'\n"
"@ withargs 1 2 3\n"
"the arguments are: 1 2 3"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:682
msgid "Equivalence with Callable Aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:684
msgid ""
"An ``ExecAlias`` is a shorthand for a callable alias. These three "
"definitions are equivalent:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../callable_aliases.rst:687
msgid ""
"@ aliases['answer'] = 'echo @(21+21)'\n"
"\n"
"@ aliases['answer'] = lambda: $[echo @(21+21)]\n"
"\n"
"@ @aliases.register\n"
"  def _answer():\n"
"      echo @(21+21)"
msgstr ""
